17:43:52 out   Build a native macOS desktop app in Rust called "Minecraft Jukebox" that works like a Minecraft jukebox, so I can listen to Minecraft music discs without opening the game.
17:43:52 out   STACK (Rust only)
17:43:52 out   - Rust 2021, single Cargo project, no web frontend, no other languages.
17:43:52 out   - GUI: eframe/egui.
17:43:52 out   - Audio: rodio with Ogg Vorbis support (symphonia or lewton), playing .ogg files directly.
17:43:52 out   - Other crates: serde + serde_json (parse Minecraft asset index), rfd (native folder picker), image (load PNG textures), dirs (find home directory), anyhow (error handling).
17:43:52 out   - Must compile with `cargo run --release` on macOS.
17:43:52 out   FEATURES
17:43:52 out   1. Disc selection: a grid with all Minecraft music discs (13, cat, blocks, chirp, far, mall, mellohi, stal, strad, ward, 11, wait, pigstep, otherside, 5, relic, creator, precipice). Each disc shows its artwork, track title and artist.
17:43:52 out   2. Jukebox: the Minecraft jukebox sits in the center. Double-clicking a disc (or dragging it onto the jukebox) "inserts" it with a small animation and the music starts, like in the game.
17:43:52 out   3. Animations: colorful music notes float out of the jukebox while playing, like in Minecraft. When the track ends, or when I press "Eject", the disc pops out.
17:43:52 out   4. Controls: eject, volume slider, progress bar with elapsed time and duration, repeat toggle, previous/next disc.
17:43:52 out   5. While a disc plays, show "Now Playing: Artist - Title" text, like in the game.
17:43:52 out   6. Keyboard shortcuts: space to play/eject, arrow keys to change disc.
17:43:52 out   VISUAL STYLE
17:43:52 out   - Fully Minecraft-styled: bundled pixel font (Press Start 2P or similar free font, embedded with include_bytes!), square borders, inventory-style panels, block-texture background (dirt, stone or wood), Minecraft-style buttons with 3D bevel look and hover state.
17:43:52 out   - All pixel art must render with nearest-neighbor filtering (no blur).
17:43:52 out   - Dark theme, resizable window with a sensible minimum size.
17:43:52 out   - If real textures are not available, draw original pixel-art discs and a jukebox procedurally in code (colored squares), one color per disc.
17:43:52 out   AUDIO AND COPYRIGHT
17:43:52 out   - Do NOT bundle any copyrighted audio files in the app.
17:43:52 out   - On first launch, ask me to select my Minecraft Java folder (default suggestion: ~/Library/Application Support/minecraft) with a native folder picker, and remember it in a small JSON config file.
17:43:52 out   - Read sounds from the assets folder: parse the newest assets/indexes/*.json, look up keys like "minecraft/sounds/records/<name>.ogg", and map each one to its file in assets/objects/<first two hash chars>/<hash>.
17:43:52 out   - If the files are not found, show a friendly screen explaining how to choose the correct folder.
17:43:52 out   - Play audio in a separate rodio sink so the UI never freezes. Track elapsed time to drive the progress bar.
17:43:52 out   CODE STRUCTURE
17:43:52 out   - Split into modules: main.rs, app.rs (egui app state and UI), disc.rs (disc list and metadata), player.rs (rodio playback), assets.rs (Minecraft folder and
